y plants their characteristic scents. As a common component of essential oils, it is something you likely encounter when smelling fresh herbs, citrus fruits, or botanical extracts.","found_in":"It is famously abundant in tea tree (Melaleuca alternifolia) and oregano (Origanum vulgare), and can also be found in everyday foods like carrots and citrus fruits.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to alpha-terpinene because of its prominent role in highly active botanical extracts like tea tree oil, exploring its potential to protect cells and fend off microbes. The strongest evidence so far comes from an individual randomised human trial (Oxford level 1b) investigating its antioxidant properties. Scientists are particularly interested in how it might work synergistically with other plant compounds to amplify these effects.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"antioxidant","statement":"May support antioxidant defenses in the body.","evidence_level":"human_trials","basis":"Based on 28 papers, including an individual randomised human trial.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"1b","label":"individual randomised trial"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"rct","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":4},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":7},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":1},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":28},{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"Is being studied for its potential to support the body's natural defenses against certain microbes.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Rests on 31 papers, primarily human observational and in vitro studies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":6},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:00